{
  "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
  "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:22 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "XIAO WANG",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "When Congress enacted ERISA, it identified a number of prohibited transactions and codified that understanding in 29 U.S.C. Section 1106. In Congress's view, these transactions posed a special risk of being potentially harmful to the plan, generally because they involved a party in interest, which includes a fiduciary's relative or an officer or an owner of the plan or a person providing services to the plan."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE THOMAS",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What -- if you were -- if we were to read your complaint as it is, what exactly is the injury?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "XIAO WANG",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The injury is that the -- with regard to the prohibited transaction provisions, Your Honor, the injury is that the plans here engaged Fidelity and TIAA, who are parties in interest, and that violates Section 1106(a)(1)(C)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0000-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you, counsel."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What are the guardrails that you think are in play here?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So you -- Rule 11 is one. Standing is another one. What do you think you have to plead to establish standing?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And what do you think about the procedure that the Solicitor General recommended involving Civil -- Rule of Civil Procedure 7?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0001-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "A district court could do that? A district court could say, after the answer is filed, I want a reply, and then rule on whether it can be determined based on the pleadings if the defendant is entitled to judgment on the pleadings?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0001-0009",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The text and structure of this statute demonstrate that the 21 exemptions in 1108(b) are the fiduciary's responsibility to plead and prove, but, as already discussed this morning, that straightforward reading raises a practical concern for the subset of claims that are at issue here, that plaintiffs could obtain discovery simply by alleging a routine service provider transaction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0001-0010",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Critically, service providers are just one of the nine categories of parties in interest. The rest are plan insiders with whom transactions carry obvious risks of favoritism and abuse."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0001-0011",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "You can pay service providers excessive fees with people's retirements money. So the scheme Congress set up set out specific transactions that are prohibited and then exemptions from those transactions that you can show that a particular transaction was reasonable and necessary."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0001-0012",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We don't think it was a mistake. We think it was entirely deliberate. And that's because, at trust law, the fiduciary had the burden to justify delegations to a third party."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0001-0013",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I don't see any textual basis here to slice and dice the way the Second Circuit suggested where it just focused on this one particular sliver of transactions."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0001-0014",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Court is inclined to do something more similar to the Second Circuit, even though I -- I can't see exactly a doctrinal basis, it wouldn't let that expand to other parties in interest."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0001-0015",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This is a straightforward prohibition and exemption structure, and these are affirmative defenses. We think that district courts already have the tools to deal with bare-bones allegations in Category 1 that we were talking before that just suggest a routine service provider transaction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0001-0016",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "to address the \"that's nuts\" example from Justice Kavanaugh, I think the way to -- to address it would be to explain that the plausibility framework precludes such complaints, and I think that's because that complaint on its face obviously implicates an affirmative defense."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We think that's sufficient to say that this explanation is not obvious."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I'm a little puzzled by what I've understood you to say, but maybe I don't understand it. Why should the plaintiff have to do anything more than plead the elements of 1106?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Earlier, Petitioners' counsel said that maybe the unreasonableness of the fee has to be pled because you have to plead an injury-in-fact. Do you accept that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0002-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Box 3 relates to the quality of the services. That's what the Second Circuit held. The Second Circuit held that it doesn't matter all this information that you've included about how excessive these fees are. This might be the Cadillac of recordkeeping plans. And if it's the Cadillac of recordkeeping plans, then those fees were justified."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 70,
        "start": 70
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: What should be pled?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0004-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 87,
        "start": 87
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. SAHARSKY: I don't think that that's true. I don't think that the law of trusts said that. And I don't think that that's -- that's something that ERISA says, particularly in the context of service provider transactions."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0004-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 87,
        "start": 87
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Now this case is not about the burden of proof. This complaint got dismissed at the pleading stage."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8abe845599872cc6dbf023b5249ebb695b10d26624891b6281e0fa74e8cc2f07",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8abe84559987-0004-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 88,
        "start": 88
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Okay. What about the Rule 7 workround?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1007",
      "confidence": 0.0,
